<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cox's Bazar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cox's Bazar is a town and district on the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, near the border with Myanmar, known for its long sandy beach on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. It is named after Hiram Cox, an officer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> who resettled Arakanese refugees there in the 1790s. Since 2017 the district has held camps sheltering about a million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohingya">Rohingya</a> refugees from Myanmar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coxs-bazar.jpg" alt="Cox's Bazar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Syedhasibulhasan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_dusk_at_Cox%27s_Bazar_sea_beach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cox's Bazar lies on a coastal plain in the south-eastern corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, about 150 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a>, bounded by the Bakkhali River on the north and east and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> on the west. Along the shore is an extensive area of beach and dunes, one of the longest natural sea beaches of South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Most of the town stands on a floodplain, so it is exposed to flooding from cyclones and storm surges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early 9th century the greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> area, including Cox's Bazar, was under the Arakan kings until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> conquered it in 1666. When the Mughal prince Shah Shuja passed through the hills on his way to Arakan he is said to have camped there; a place named Dulahazara, meaning one thousand palanquins, recalls his retinue. After the Mughals the area came under the Tipras and the Arakanese, then the Portuguese and then the British.</p><p>The name comes from Captain Hiram Cox, an officer of the British East India Company who was superintendent of the outpost of Palonki. He took on the settlement of Arakanese refugees and died in 1799 before finishing the work, and a market named after him commemorated this. The town was first established in 1854 and became a municipality in 1869. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a> of 1971 Pakistani soldiers killed many people in the town. The subdivision became a district in 1984, and in 2017 hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohingya">Rohingya</a> refugees from Myanmar arrived in the district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cox's Bazar is a fishing port and tourism centre and the headquarters of its district; its population was 196,385 in the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Nearby are Himchari National Park, established in 1980, and the Buddhist monastery of Aggmeda Khyang. The town is linked by road and rail to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cox's Bazar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The persecution of the Rohingya led The Gambia to bring a genocide case against Myanmar at the International Court of Justice in 2019 and created one of the world's largest refugee camps, at <b>Cox's Bazar</b> in Bangladesh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
